      "type": "llm_response",
      "reasoning": "Okay, we've mulliganed twice, and now we're looking at what will become our 6-card opening hand. The game presented me with 7 cards and asked me to put one on the bottom of my library. This is how the \"Vancouver Mulligan\" rule works after you've mulliganed.\n\
\n\
Let's examine the hand:\n\
\n\
**Lands (5):**\n\
*   Vivid Creek (enters tapped, but can produce any color later)\n\
*   Island (x3)\n\
*   Mountain\n\
\n\
That's 5 lands, which is excellent for a 6-card hand. We have solid mana production for our colors (Blue and Red).\n\
\n\
**Spells (2):**\n\
*   Windfall ({2}{U}) - A 3-mana spell that makes all players discard their hand and draw a new hand equal to the largest number discarded. This is a powerful \"wheel\" effect.\n\
*   Thought Reflection ({4}{U}{U}) - A 6-mana enchantment that doubles our card draws.\n\
\n\
**The Decision:** I need to pick one card to put on the bottom.\n\
\n\
If I keep Thought Reflection and put Windfall on the bottom, I'm left with a 6-mana spell as my only non-land permanent. That's far too slow for an opening hand, especially after mulliganing. We want to be able to *do* something within the first few turns.\n\
\n\
However, if I put **Thought Reflection** on the bottom, I'm left with Windfall. Windfall costs {2}{U}, which we can cast as early as turn 3 with our land base (Island turn 1, Island turn 2, Mountain or Vivid Creek turn 3). A turn 3 Windfall can be incredibly disruptive and help us reload our smaller hand. This is a much stronger opening for a 6-card hand. While Thought Reflection is powerful, it's a late-game card that doesn't help us stabilize or advance our board early.\n\
\n\
Therefore, the optimal play is to put **Thought Reflection** on the bottom.\n\
\n\
